{"reasoning": "The deliverables fully satisfy criterion GO009. The conference_budget.xlsx Daily sheet explicitly tracks fiscal year version selection with a `fiscal_year` column (2025 for 9/28-9/30, 2026 for 10/1-10/2) and a `source` column identifying which workbook was used (FY2025 vs FY2026), with specific Excel row locators documented in the Sources sheet. The actual rates (lodging_cap, MIE_full) are identical across both fiscal years for each city (e.g., Chicago: lodging 223, MIE 92; Denver: lodging 215, MIE 92; Phoenix: lodging 160, MIE 86). The approval_brief.docx explicitly states in the \"\u89c4\u5219\u548c\u8bc1\u636e\" section: \"\u4e24\u8d22\u653f\u5e74\u5ea6\u5728\u672c\u9898\u4e09\u5730\u65e5\u671f\u7684\u4f4f\u5bbf\u53caM&IE\u8d39\u7387\u672a\u53d1\u751f\u53d8\u5316\uff1b\u4e0d\u540c\u7248\u672c\u4ecd\u987b\u5206\u522b\u5339\u914d\" (the lodging and M&IE rates for the three cities did not change across the two fiscal years; different versions still need to be matched separately). This directly explains the version selection rationale and confirms no rate change, without inferring a price increase from the version change. The approval_register.csv provides supporting documentation but the key evidence is in the budget workbook and approval brief.", "score": "yes"}

